---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import groupings from '../../generated/groupings.json';
import { longDate, unseenChangeCount, type Reassignment } from '../../lib/groupings.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

interface Grouping {
  number: number;
  slug: string;
  name: string;
  members: string[];
  verified: boolean;
}

const doc = groupings as {
  status: string;
  act: { name: string; citation: { statute: string; verified: boolean }; text_url: string | null };
  reassignment?: Reassignment | null;
  groupings: Grouping[];
};

// The groups below are the law's. The state's guides have since redrawn them,
// and we hold only one of the moves -- so this page has to lead with that, not
// bury it. See site/src/lib/groupings.ts.
const moved = doc.reassignment ?? null;
const unseen = unseenChangeCount(moved);
const source = moved?.sources?.[0] ?? null;
---

<Base title="Act 170 groupings — Vermont School Budgets">
  <h1>Act 170 groups</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The groups of school districts named in {doc.act?.name ?? 'Act 170'}. These are the starting
    what-ifs in the tool. For most people, the real question is not "what if we merged with
    someone?" but "what happens under the group the Legislature already put us in?"
    {moved && <span> The law let the state's guides move districts between groups. They have.
    Read the warning below before you use this list.</span>}
  </p>

  {moved && !moved.roster_published && (
    <div class="notice blocking">
      <strong>These groups have changed. We do not have the new list.</strong>
      <p>
        On {longDate(moved.as_of)} the {moved.by} — the group the state hired to run the merger
        talks — put Vermont's {moved.district_count ?? 'roughly 119'} school districts into
        <strong> {moved.committee_count ?? 'fewer'} groups</strong>, not the {doc.groupings.length} the
        law printed. {moved.requests_granted ?? 'Some'} districts had asked to move, and got their
        way. The law always allowed this.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>They have not published which districts are in which new group.</strong> We know
        about {moved.known_changes.length} of the {moved.requests_granted ?? 'granted'} moves, from
        news reports. {unseen !== null && unseen > 0 && (
          <span>That leaves {unseen} moves we cannot see.</span>
        )}
      </p>
      <p>
        So the groups below are still the law's groups, exactly as it wrote them. For many
        districts that is still the right answer. For at least one it is not, and we cannot tell
        you which of the rest changed. If a group's page says nothing about a change, that means
        we have no report about it — not that nothing happened.
      </p>
      {moved.known_changes.length > 0 && (
        <>
          <p>What we do know:</p>
          <ul>
            {moved.known_changes.map((c) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{c.district_name_as_written ?? c.district}</strong> moved
                {c.from_grouping !== null && (
                  <span> out of <a href={`${base}/groupings/${c.from_grouping}/`}>group {c.from_grouping}</a></span>
                )}
                {c.to_grouping_as_reported && <span> and in {c.to_grouping_as_reported}</span>}
                {c.to_grouping !== null && c.to_grouping_basis === 'inferred' && (
                  <span> — which looks like <a href={`${base}/groupings/${c.to_grouping}/`}>group {c.to_grouping}</a>, though the guides have not numbered the new groups, so that match is our reading and could be wrong</span>
                )}.
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </>
      )}
      {moved.status === 'announced_provisional' && (
        <p>
          The guides called the new groups a starting point, not a decision. School board leaders
          could send comments until {longDate(moved.comment_deadline)}, and the plan was to settle
          the groups on {longDate(moved.finalize_target)}.
          {moved.first_meeting_deadline && (
            <span> Each group has to hold its first meeting by {longDate(moved.first_meeting_deadline)}.</span>
          )}
        </p>
      )}
      {source && (
        <p style="font-size:.9rem">
          Where this comes from: <a href={source.url}>{source.publisher}, "{source.title}"</a>,
          {' '}{longDate(source.published_date)}. That is a news report about the guides'
          announcement, not the announcement itself. Nobody here has checked it against the guides'
          own list, because we have not been able to get one.
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  )}

  {
    doc.groupings.length === 0 ? (
      <div class="notice blocking">
        <strong>Not typed in yet</strong>
        <p>
          This list is empty because we have not typed the groups in yet — not because the law has
          none. The law does have them, and they matter for everything this tool does.
        </p>
        <p>
          No public data feed lists them, and we could not reach the Legislature's website when
          this site was built. Writing out twenty groups of district names from memory would risk
          putting a made-up answer in the one place people most need the truth. Someone on this
          page is asking which districts the Legislature grouped them with, and a confident wrong
          answer there is much worse than no answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Typing them in is a small, careful job of copying from the law's own text. When it is
          done, each group will show the district names exactly as the law writes them, next to the
          IDs we match them to, so the match can be checked. Names in the law and names used by the
          Agency do not always agree, and any name we cannot match will be listed, not dropped.
        </p>
      </div>
    ) : (
      <ul class="grid">
        {doc.groupings.map((g) => (
          <li class="card">
            <h3 style="margin-top:0">
              <a href={`${base}/groupings/${g.number}/`}>
                {g.number}. {g.name}
              </a>
            </h3>
            <p style="margin:.3rem 0 0;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--text-muted)">
              {g.members.length} district(s)
              {!g.verified && <span class="tag unverified" style="margin-left:.5rem">not checked</span>}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )
  }

  <h2>Where this comes from</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Law</dt>
    <dd>{doc.act?.citation?.statute ?? '—'}</dd>
    <dt>Checked against the law</dt>
    <dd>
      {doc.act?.citation?.verified ? 'yes' : <span class="tag unverified">not checked yet</span>}
    </dd>
  </dl>
</Base>
